      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Preemptive military intervention by democratic nations is a morally fraught and deeply contested issue, and a definitive framework is elusive. One could argue from a utilitarian perspective that preventing a future, greater harm caused by an authoritarian regime justifies the immediate, smaller cost of intervention. Conversely, a deontological view emphasizes the inherent wrongness of initiating violence, regardless of the perceived good outcome, thus rejecting preemptive strikes as a violation of sovereignty. Ultimately, the justification hinges on whether one prioritizes preventing catastrophic future suffering or upholding the absolute principle of non-aggression.",
